from reportlab.lib import colors
from reportlab.lib.styles import ParagraphStyle
from reportlab.platypus import SimpleDocTemplate, Paragraph
from reportlab.lib.pagesizes import letter
from pypdf import PdfReader

paragraphs = [
'''Github Repo Link: https://github.com/em3ndo/verivoice-ai.git''',
'''Suppose that a customer calls their bank, repeats a verification phrase, and is allowed to continue. The bank has checked the caller's voice, but that check only describes the person who spoke at the beginning. Another person may take over, or an impersonator may attempt to pass verification using recorded or AI-generated speech. Checking the voice throughout the conversation thus becomes an appealing idea, and the one that motivated my development of VeriVoice AI. I built the project for customer-service providers and their callers, with support for English, Spanish, Hindi, and Russian. Voice is useful here because the caller is already supplying the information needed for repeated checks simply by speaking.''',
'''To explain how the protocol works, I first describe how the system obtains a reference for the user's voice. The user creates a password-protected account and reads out-loud five sentences generated by Gemini in their selected language. I use Deepgram to transcribe each recording and detect its language, then Gemini to check whether the transcript matches the sentence. Hiya checks for synthetic speech, requiring a non-synthetic score of at least 0.50 before accepting a recording. The five accepted recordings are then used to compute a voiceprint, which future speech is compared against. Each account may have a separate voiceprint for each supported language.''',
'''Now suppose that the enrolled user begins a call. The backend collects short speech windows and sends each window to Hiya and Soniox simultaneously. Hiya evaluates similarity to the enrolled voice as a score A (for "authenticity" to the user's identity), evidence of synthetic speech as a score S, and evidence of replay as a score R. Soniox provides language labels for transcribed tokens, allowing me to calculate the fraction L which match the selected language. Tokens with uncertain transcriptions and recognized project brand names are excluded when calculating L. I chose these APIs for their distinct roles: Hiya supplies voice verification, pre-recorded voice detection, and AI-deepfake detection; Deepgram handles completed enrollment recordings; Soniox provides the language information needed during the call. Gemini supplies the spoken conversation, while the backend independently determines whether the call may continue. I used both Soniox and Deepgram because I found out during development that the latter offers no real-time language identification.''',
'''These observations must then be combined into a decision. I define "humanness" H as the minimum of the replay score R and the adjusted non-synthetic score S_adj = S + 0.5(1 - S). Real-time scores for Humanness H, authenticity A, and language consistency L are each smoothed by an algorithm which calculates the exponential moving average C_H, C_A, C_L respectively by giving the newest observation and previous score equal weight (i.e. a decay constant of 0.5); the overall confidence C is the minimum of these three smoothed values. For instance, a recording may closely resemble the enrolled speaker but receive a low replay score. Taking the minimum prevents the strong identity match from hiding the latter concern. Initial confidence C of at least 0.70 permits conversation, instantaneous C values strictly between 0.70 and 0.80 produce periodic warnings, and any instantaneous C value below 0.70 ends the call. Missing verification evidence also ends the call.''',
'''I implemented this protocol with a Python/FastAPI backend, SQLite storage, and an HTML/CSS/JavaScript interface which streams microphone audio through WebSockets. The demonstration lets the user speak with a pretend banker at the imaginary Satoshi Bank; all banking actions are fictional. The voice reference can also adapt: recordings whose raw identity, synthesis, replay, and language scores all reach 0.70, and which meet the voice-duration requirement, may join the original enrollment clips in a replacement voiceprint after the call. That replacement voiceprint is used only after successful computation. This updates the account owner's voiceprint with new voice data without retraining Hiya's underlying model.''',
'''My next step would be to measure false acceptance, false rejection, and response time across genuine callers, AI or recorded-audio spoof attempts, and unexpected languages. These measurements are necessary to justify the current thresholds and to prevent falsely accepted speech from entering the adaptive voiceprint. VeriVoice currently demonstrates the protocol locally; its confidence score is not a calibrated probability, and enrolling a voice does not establish legal identity or phone ownership. Testing those limits would determine how far the prototype can progress toward its intended customer-service use.'''
]
path='output/pdf/verivoice-project-overview-personal-style.pdf'
style=ParagraphStyle('Body',fontName='Times-Roman',fontSize=11,leading=13.2,spaceAfter=8,textColor=colors.HexColor('#202020'))
title=ParagraphStyle('Title',fontName='Times-Bold',fontSize=17,leading=21,spaceAfter=16,alignment=1)
doc=SimpleDocTemplate(path,pagesize=letter,rightMargin=49,leftMargin=49,topMargin=39,bottomMargin=39,title='VeriVoice AI: Continuous Voice Verification')
doc.build([Paragraph('VeriVoice AI: Adaptive Voice Verification',title)]+[Paragraph(p,style) for p in paragraphs])
r=PdfReader(path)
print('Pages:',len(r.pages),'Words:',len(' '.join(paragraphs).split()))
assert len(r.pages)==1
